1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:03,626
Here's how to reverse engineer Expensify from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,676 --> 00:00:08,265
We gave Claude one Markdown file, and checked twenty-four things in a real browser.

3
00:00:08,315 --> 00:00:10,222
Here's what passed, and what broke.

4
00:00:11,067 --> 00:00:15,329
The spec copies the part of Expensify a small team uses to pay people back.

5
00:00:15,379 --> 00:00:16,996
Expenses with their receipts.

6
00:00:17,046 --> 00:00:19,698
Category limits, and mileage at a company rate.

7
00:00:19,748 --> 00:00:21,423
One approval, by your manager.

8
00:00:21,473 --> 00:00:24,757
And a finance queue, where every payment gets a reference.

9
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,907
The core rule is the report's lifecycle.

10
00:00:27,957 --> 00:00:29,498
A report starts as a draft.

11
00:00:29,548 --> 00:00:31,620
Submitting sends it to your manager.

12
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:34,920
Approved reports go to finance, and reimbursed is final.

13
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:37,866
Before you can submit, every flag needs an answer.

14
00:00:37,916 --> 00:00:43,052
A missing receipt blocks the report, and an expense over its limit needs an explanation.

15
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:46,772
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

16
00:00:46,822 --> 00:00:49,817
The foundation, with sign-in, people and settings.

17
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:51,522
Reports, expenses and flags.

18
00:00:51,572 --> 00:00:52,800
Approval and payment.

19
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:54,505
Then reminders and spending.

20
00:00:54,555 --> 00:00:59,680
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

21
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:01,689
Here's what it built.

22
00:01:01,739 --> 00:01:06,513
Lee's draft report flags a dinner over the meals limit, and a hotel with no receipt.

23
00:01:06,563 --> 00:01:09,786
Finance sees every report, with its status and next step.

24
00:01:09,836 --> 00:01:12,715
Spending adds it all up, by category and by person.

25
00:01:12,765 --> 00:01:15,988
Settings hold the receipt threshold and the mileage rate.

26
00:01:16,038 --> 00:01:20,180
And on a phone, every expense is a card, and Take photo opens the camera.

27
00:01:21,033 --> 00:01:27,096
In the final run, on a fresh database, all twenty-four checks passed, along with fifty-nine automated tests.

28
00:01:27,933 --> 00:01:30,190
But the more useful result is what broke.

29
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:32,441
The build exposed nine gaps in the spec.

30
00:01:32,491 --> 00:01:34,297
Places where Claude had to guess.

31
00:01:34,347 --> 00:01:38,292
The seed data loads relative to today, but the checks used fixed dates.

32
00:01:38,342 --> 00:01:42,062
Two parts of the spec named different people who can see a receipt.

33
00:01:42,112 --> 00:01:46,394
And changing a report's approver had no event, no email, and no comment rule.

34
00:01:46,444 --> 00:01:48,363
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

35
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:52,391
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

36
00:01:52,441 --> 00:01:58,990
We didn't test real sign-in, sending email, the hourly job on a real scheduler, a real phone camera, or hosting.

37
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:02,054
Those are yours to check before a team relies on it.

38
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:04,156
So is it worth building?

39
00:02:04,206 --> 00:02:07,803
Expensify's Submit plan is free, with receipt scanning and mileage.

40
00:02:07,853 --> 00:02:12,429
Reimbursements and approvals come with Collect, at five dollars per member per month.

41
00:02:12,479 --> 00:02:15,749
If you want receipt scanning or company cards, use Expensify.

42
00:02:15,799 --> 00:02:22,390
If you reimburse out-of-pocket spending for a small team, you mostly need receipts, one approval, and a record of payment.

43
00:02:23,233 --> 00:02:24,193
The spec is free.

44
00:02:24,243 --> 00:02:31,324
The data model, twelve rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty-four checks, with the nine fixes already in.

45
00:02:31,374 --> 00:02:33,998
Find it at personal software factory dot com.
